  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Month as a Map",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Month as a Map\"**\n\nThe month is a map of where the money went: category tiles sized by spending, each with a thin limit rail. Everything else, the forms, lists and menus, stays plain so the map is the one loud thing on any screen. The tool is used two ways: one-handed at a till, in shop daylight, and in the evening under a kitchen lamp. It is a family's working ledger, not a fintech dashboard, and it refuses the white-card dashboard with a hero metric and a donut.\n\nThe palette is one light, warm set: a stone ground, near-black ink, and eight deep earthy hues that exist only to colour category tiles. Weight is spent on amounts (800) and almost nowhere else; type is the system sans with tabular numerals everywhere, so columns of money line up. Depth is flat: the screen is separated by tone and hairlines, not shadow. The sub-pages (entries, entry form, pocket, settings) are plain forms and hairline rows on the same stone ground; none of them borrows the map's colour.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One light theme, tuned for daylight and lamplight; no dark theme.",
      "Colour carries category identity (tiles); ink-black carries the single action.",
      "Money is tabular, heavy and tight; labels are plain and quiet.",
      "Thumb-first: 48px targets, capture dock fixed at the bottom of the home screen, wide full-width submit buttons on forms.",
      "Offline-aware: one queue counter floats on every page; refused queue items are shown, not dropped.",
      "Hungarian only; strings live in `app/strings/hu.py`, never in templates."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Map Owns Colour Rule",
        "body": "Saturated colour appears only as a category tile hue. Chrome, buttons, rows and menus are stone and ink.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Stable Hue Rule",
        "body": "A category's hue is a function of its id (`c{id % 8}`), never of its rank or size, so it does not change between months or screens.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Not-Red-For-Over Rule",
        "body": "Brick is a category hue, so red cannot mean \"over limit\". Over-limit is a hatched limit rail plus bold \"+N € felett\" text on the tile. `--error` is for form errors and refused queue items only.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Heavy Amounts Rule",
        "body": "Weight 800 is reserved for money (statement balance, tile amounts, amount input, login title). Names and labels stay at 500 to 700.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Step-Down Rule",
        "body": "Type shrinks by scale step, never below 0.6875rem; a narrow tile (container under 124px) drops name to xs and amount to m, and under 92px it also tightens its padding to 8px and drops the name to 0.6875rem, before a word breaks.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Whole Euros On Tiles Rule",
        "body": "Tiles show whole euros (`eur_short`) because they are glanceable; lists and totals show exact cents (`eur`).",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Limit Yields First Rule",
        "body": "A short tile (`tile--small`) hides its limit line before the hidden-entry note, because the note is part of the truth of the number and the limit is still visible on the rail.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Counter Floats Rule",
        "body": "The offline queue counter exists once per page, fixed and centred at the bottom; where a capture dock exists it sits 92px above the bottom edge, clear of the dock, otherwise 16px.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat-By-Default Rule",
        "body": "Surfaces carry no shadow at rest; only elements that float over scrolling content (the menu popover, the queue counter) are lifted.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** use ink `#1c1b19` for the single primary action on a screen, and the tile hues only for categories.",
      "**Do** assign category hue by `c{id % 8}` and keep \"Egyéb\" neutral.",
      "**Do** put tabular numerals on every amount and weight 800 only on amounts.",
      "**Do** show whole euros on tiles and exact cents in lists.",
      "**Do** signal over-limit with the hatched rail and \"+N € felett\" text.",
      "**Do** keep touch targets at 48px or more and every string in `app/strings/hu.py`.",
      "**Do** make secondary actions quiet buttons (hairline outline), so the ink button stays the single action.",
      "**Do** use a segmented choice for two to three exclusive options and a `details` row for anything that needs a form but is rarely opened.",
      "**Do** pick palette decisions from the scene: shop daylight and kitchen lamp, one light theme."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** add a dark theme; one tuned palette beats two half-tuned ones.",
      "**Don't** use red, or `--error`, for over-limit, deletions or counts; it is for form errors and refused queue items only.",
      "**Don't** drop a tile's hidden-entry note to keep its limit line; the limit goes first.",
      "**Don't** add a hero-metric card, a donut chart, or white dashboard cards around the map.",
      "**Don't** use `--ink-3` for text people must read; it is disabled-only.",
      "**Don't** introduce a webfont or a second family; the system sans is a recorded decision.",
      "**Don't** add a second floating element or a second queue counter; one chip per page.",
      "**Don't** reuse the repo owner's `brand/` identity; this is the family's tool."
    ]
  }
}
